  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=4\\sqrt{x}-4x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\sqrt{x}-4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{1/2}-4x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{\u2032}(1)=2(1)^{-1/2}-4=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(0) & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y & =-2x+2-0 \\\\ y & =-2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2(0)+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $(0,2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
